<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Japanese occupation of British Borneo</h1><p class="st-meta">1941 CE – 1945 CE · capital Kuching</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Japanese military rule of Sarawak, Brunei, Labuan and North Borneo during the Second World War, ending in the Sandakan death marches.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Japanese forces landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miri">Miri</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/raj-of-sarawak">Sarawak</a> on 16 December 1941, seeking its oilfields, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuching">Kuching</a> on 24 December. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crown-colony-of-labuan">Labuan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/north-borneo">North Borneo</a> fell by January 1942. The former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> of Sarawak, Brunei and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah">North Borneo</a> were united as Kita Boruneo under the Imperial Japanese Army, with headquarters at Kuching.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The occupiers ran the oilfields and requisitioned food and labour, causing shortages. Chinese communities suffered reprisals, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-kinabalu">Jesselton</a> revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-kwok">Albert Kwok</a> in October 1943 was crushed with mass executions. Prisoners of war and civilians were held at camps such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-lintang-camp">Batu Lintang</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuching">Kuching</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1945 Allied prisoners at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandakan">Sandakan</a> were forced on death marches to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranau">Ranau</a>, and only six of some 2,400 survived. Australian forces landed at Tarakan, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crown-colony-of-labuan">Labuan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei</a> Bay from May to July 1945, and the Japanese surrendered in September. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> then created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crown-colony-of-sarawak">Crown Colony of Sarawak</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/crown-colony-of-north-borneo">Crown Colony of North Borneo</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/japanese-occupation-of-british-borneo.jpg" alt="Japanese occupation of British Borneo" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: kahusi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan_(1870%E2%80%931999).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1941 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuching</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Toshinari Maeda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, traditional religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a>, Brunei</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kita Boruneo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1943&amp;focus=japanese-occupation-of-british-borneo" aria-label="Japanese occupation of British Borneo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7745 2119 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Japanese occupation of British Borneo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8065 2375L8178 2269M8065 2375L8065 2375M8065 2375L8273 2215M8065 2375L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8178 2269h0M8065 2375h0M8273 2215h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8065 2375h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8065 2375h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kuching. <a href="/atlas?year=1943&amp;focus=japanese-occupation-of-british-borneo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

